    <ShortLegislativeSummaryEn>&lt;div&gt;A legislative summary is currently being prepared for this bill by the Parliamentary Information and Research Service of the Library of Parliament. Meanwhile, the following executive summary is available.&lt;br/&gt;On 10 June 2010, the Minister of citizenship, immigration and multiculturalism introduced Bill C-37, the Strengthening the Value of Canadian Citizenship Act, in the House of Commons and it was given first reading.&lt;br/&gt;The bill amends the Citizenship Act. Among other things it:&lt;br/&gt;•	Amends certain conditions relating to the granting of and eligibility for citizenship in certain cases:&lt;br/&gt;-death of a parent who had not yet been granted citizenship.&lt;br/&gt;–grandchildren of Canadians who have served abroad in or with the Canadian armed forces or the federal public administration and other related exemptions.&lt;br/&gt;–citizenship may not be acquired after the first generation either by descent or in the case of adoption.&lt;br/&gt;-applicants for citizenship must be present in Canada for a specified period.&lt;br/&gt;&lt;br/&gt;•	Provides for new processes respecting the revocation of citizenship in the case of false representation or fraud and declarations of inadmissibility leading to the deportation of the person whose citizenship is revoked.&lt;br/&gt;•	Provides for the regulation of third-party involvement in the citizenship process.&lt;br/&gt;•	Expands the prohibitions with respect to grants of citizenship with respect to foreign offences and sentences served outside Canada.&lt;/div&gt;</ShortLegislativeSummaryEn>
    <ShortLegislativeSummaryFr>&lt;div&gt;Le Service d'information et de recherche parlementaires de la Bibliothèque du Parlement rédige actuellement le résumé législatif de ce projet de loi. Entre-temps, le Service met à votre disposition le sommaire suivant.&lt;br/&gt;Le 10 juin 2010, le ministre de la citoyenneté, de l'immigration et du multiculturalisme a déposé le projet de loi C-37 : Loi valorisant la citoyenneté canadienne, à la Chambre des communes en première lecture.&lt;br/&gt;Le projet de loi modifie la Loi sur la citoyenneté. Il a notamment pour effet :&lt;br/&gt;•	De modifier certaines conditions quant à l'obtention et à l'admissibilité à la citoyenneté dans certains cas :&lt;br/&gt;-décès d'un parent n'ayant pas encore la qualité de citoyen.&lt;br/&gt;–Des petits enfants nés à l'étranger des Canadiens au service des forces armées ou de l'administration publique fédérale et de certaines exceptions s'y rattachant.&lt;br/&gt;–Que la citoyenneté ne peut être acquise après la première génération ni par filiation ni en cas d'adoption.&lt;br/&gt;-Que le demandeur d'une demande de citoyenneté doit être présent au Canada pour une période spécifique.&lt;br/&gt;&lt;br/&gt;•	De prévoir de nouveaux processus au niveau de la révocation de la citoyenneté dans les cas de fraudes ou de fausses déclarations ainsi qu'au niveau d'une demande de déclaration d'interdiction de territoire visant l'expulsion de la personne visée par la révocation.&lt;br/&gt;•	De préciser le rôle d'un tiers intervenant dans le processus d'obtention de la citoyenneté.&lt;br/&gt;•	D'élargir la portée des interdictions d'octroi de la citoyenneté au niveau des crimes commis à l'étranger et des peines purgées à l'étranger.&lt;/div&gt;</ShortLegislativeSummaryFr>
    <ShortLegislativeSummary>&lt;div&gt;A legislative summary is currently being prepared for this bill by the Parliamentary Information and Research Service of the Library of Parliament. Meanwhile, the following executive summary is available.&lt;br/&gt;On 10 June 2010, the Minister of citizenship, immigration and multiculturalism introduced Bill C-37, the Strengthening the Value of Canadian Citizenship Act, in the House of Commons and it was given first reading.&lt;br/&gt;The bill amends the Citizenship Act. Among other things it:&lt;br/&gt;•	Amends certain conditions relating to the granting of and eligibility for citizenship in certain cases:&lt;br/&gt;-death of a parent who had not yet been granted citizenship.&lt;br/&gt;–grandchildren of Canadians who have served abroad in or with the Canadian armed forces or the federal public administration and other related exemptions.&lt;br/&gt;–citizenship may not be acquired after the first generation either by descent or in the case of adoption.&lt;br/&gt;-applicants for citizenship must be present in Canada for a specified period.&lt;br/&gt;&lt;br/&gt;•	Provides for new processes respecting the revocation of citizenship in the case of false representation or fraud and declarations of inadmissibility leading to the deportation of the person whose citizenship is revoked.&lt;br/&gt;•	Provides for the regulation of third-party involvement in the citizenship process.&lt;br/&gt;•	Expands the prohibitions with respect to grants of citizenship with respect to foreign offences and sentences served outside Canada.&lt;/div&gt;</ShortLegislativeSummary>
